  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "v10.0.8 (SpawnDev fork): DTLS client cipher-suite fix. DtlsClient.GetSupportedCipherSuites() used to branch on OUR certificate's signature algorithm and offer only that auth family (RSA cert -> only TLS_ECDHE_RSA_*, ECDSA cert -> only TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_*). A TLS client's cipher-suite list describes the certificate it will ACCEPT FROM THE PEER, not its own, so this rejected every peer whose certificate was the other type and the handshake died with handshake_failure(40) after ICE had already connected - which reads as a network fault and is not one. It now offers the union of both auth families (5 ECDSA + 5 RSA suites). handshake_failure(40) also now logs our certificate type and the suites we offered instead of a bare alert. MEASURED against the Reachy Mini robot: RSA peer 992 packets (was already working), ECDSA peer 0 packets before the fix and 990 packets after. v10.0.7 (SpawnDev fork): Answerer DTLS-role fix. When RTCPeerConnection receives an OFFER it now honors the offerer's a=setup per RFC 4145 §4.1 / RFC 5763 §5 (offer setup:active -> answer setup:passive; setup:passive -> active; setup:actpass/absent -> active as before) instead of unconditionally answering setup:active. The old behavior produced two DTLS clients (both sending ClientHello -> unexpected_message(10)) against an offerer that explicitly requested setup:active, e.g. the SpawnWear watch / libpeer which forces itself to DTLS client. No change for the common setup:actpass browser-offer path. v10.0.6 (SpawnDev fork): Single additive change on top of 10.0.5 - `MediaStreamTrack.StreamStatus` setter widened from `internal` to `public`. Lets consumers like `SpawnDev.MultiMedia` set the W3C `MediaStreamTrack.enabled`-equivalent state from outside the `SIPSorcery.*` namespace without reflection or wrapper indirection. Zero behavior change otherwise; the field's existing internal callers still set it the same way. Source: commit `c4b5f79e8` on the LostBeard/sipsorcery fork. Ships with the SpawnDev.RTC 1.1.8 stable cut so consumers picking up the RTC rollup get the widening transitively. v10.0.5 changelog (Production-TURN extensions: TurnServerConfig.ResolveHmacKey + RelayPortRangeStart/End, SctpAssociation.MaxBurst + BurstPeriodMilliseconds, SctpDataSender producer-consumer lost-wakeup race fix) is still in 10.0.6 - this is purely additive.",
